# Docker Image Versioning

## Core Principle

**Never hardcode a version in compose/Dockerfile. Parameterize the tag (default `latest`) and stamp the real version from git at build time.** Dev builds stay `latest`; release builds carry the git version. The compose file is written once and never edited per release.

## Tag scheme (computed from git)

- HEAD is exactly on a tag → use the tag (`git describe --tags --exact-match`)
- otherwise → `sha-<8>` (`git rev-parse --short=8 HEAD`)
- working tree dirty (tracked or untracked) → append `-dirty` (`git status --porcelain` non-empty)

## compose

```yaml
services:
  myapp:
    image: myapp:${VERSION:-latest}   # default latest; build feeds VERSION
    build: ...
```

## Makefile (compose can't run git, so compute outside and pass it in)

```makefile
VERSION := $(shell \
	v=$$(git describe --tags --exact-match 2>/dev/null || echo sha-$$(git rev-parse --short=8 HEAD)); \
	[ -z "$$(git status --porcelain)" ] || v=$$v-dirty; \
	echo $$v)

build:
	VERSION=$(VERSION) docker compose build
```

- `docker compose build` (no make) → `:latest`
- `make build` → `:v0.1.0` on a tagged commit, else `:sha-1a2b3c4d`, else `...-dirty`

## CI (the canonical source of truth is the git tag)

- Build/push release images in CI, not from a dev box.
- Derive tags from the git ref (`docker/metadata-action` on GitHub Actions): a real tag → `:1.2.3` + `:latest`; branch/PR → `:main` / `:sha-…`.
- Reuse the same git-version logic as the Makefile so local and CI agree.

❌ Avoid: editing `image:` per release, or shipping only `:latest` (unpinnable, not reproducible).
✅ Correct: one parameterized `image:`, version injected from git, releases tagged both `:X.Y.Z` and `:latest`.
